    <bf:tableOfContents>
      <bf:TableOfContents>
	<rdfs:label>Introduction -- Living on the Edge: Exiles and their Communities -- Tall Tales and Long Travels: Dialogs of Disbelief Between the Defendants and Authorities -- "The Court's Mercy": Crime, Punishment, and Early Modern Prison-life -- A Hard Break: Exile and Return -- Pack the Family: The Effects of Exile and Criminal Sentencing on Children and Families -- Walk the Line: Pilgrimage, Disability, and Violence -- Life Unbound: Exile and Banditry in the Early Modern Mediterranean -- Epilogue.</rdfs:label>
      </bf:TableOfContents>
    </bf:tableOfContents>
    <bf:summary>
      <bf:Summary>
	<rdfs:label>"Early modern Spanish courts frequently sentenced poor convicts and unhoused migrants with felony exile or banishment, essentially evicting them from towns and provinces. This book uncovers the lives of poor travelers before, during, and after sentencing to help understand their experiences of poverty, mobility, and criminal sentencing"-- Provided by publisher.</rdfs:label>
      </bf:Summary>
    </bf:summary>
    <dcterms:isPartOf rdf:resource="http://id.loc.gov/resources/works"/>
